data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Clarifying the Rule Types**\n\nI'm now focusing on specific rule types. I've realized that a sum-based rule, $\\phi(X) = \\mathbf{1}\\{\\sum_{i=1}^n g_i(X_i) \u003e t\\}$, doesn't guarantee equal expectations under different distributions, as the indicator function introduces nonlinearity, and the variance and distribution of the sum depend on the joint law. I'm exploring randomized rules, specifically marginally additive ones. It seems that additive randomized rules, $\\phi(X) = \\sum_{i=1}^n w_i \\psi_i(X_i)$, would make the expectations identical. I'm also confirming deterministic rules work for additive rules in a similar manner.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1006,"totalTokenCount": 2784,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1006}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1778,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "1P8Ca6WuO-PGxdIP2cO30Qk"}

