data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Constructing Counterexamples**\n\nI'm now building clear, finite-$n$ counterexamples using Bernoulli($1/2$) distributions. I've set $n=2$ with $X_1$ and $X_2$ marginally identical. I'm demonstrating how a max rule, $\\phi_{\\max}$, and the average, $\\bar{X}$, yield different expectations under $P$ (independent) and $Q$ (perfectly correlated), thus showing detection is possible despite identical marginals. I'm also planning to explore if symmetric rules can distinguish them.\n\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1006,"totalTokenCount": 3610,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1006}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 2604,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "1P8Ca6WuO-PGxdIP2cO30Qk"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Symmetric Rules**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on permutation-invariant rules. I've found an interesting case: When the empirical distribution (unordered multiset) remains identical under both $P$ and $Q$, and I can see that this means symmetric rules will yield identical expectations under both scenarios. This suggests that non-symmetric rules would be able to distinguish between them, so I'll be investigating this further. Specifically, I'm considering $(0,1,2)$ cyclic shifts versus reversed cyclic shifts to test this.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1006,"totalTokenCount": 4037,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1006}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 3031,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "1P8Ca6WuO-PGxdIP2cO30Qk"}

