data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Confirming Marginal Additivity**\n\nI've just had a major realization regarding marginally additive rules. I can now rigorously demonstrate that a deterministic rule $\\phi$ cannot be marginally additive, by contradiction. The proof stems from showing how two non-constant $\\psi_i$ functions in the rule inevitably lead to at least three distinct values within the binary range $\\{0,1\\}$, making it impossible. This is a crucial finding that refines my understanding of expectation behavior.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1006,"totalTokenCount": 4856,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1006}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 3850,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "1P8Ca6WuO-PGxdIP2cO30Qk"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Demonstrating Single-Coordinate Rules**\n\nI am now focusing on the implications for deterministic rules. My analysis now strongly suggests that any such rule, to remain \"blind,\" must only depend on a maximum of one coordinate, and cannot be marginally additive! It contradicts the implication from two non-constant functions in the rule inevitably lead to at least three distinct values, which are impossible for a $\\{0,1\\}$ output.\n\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1006,"totalTokenCount": 5147,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1006}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 4141,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "1P8Ca6WuO-PGxdIP2cO30Qk"}

