data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Statistical Considerations**\n\nI'm now carefully examining the implications of a finite sample, especially regarding dependence tests. Calibration of thresholds is impossible without prior information (e.g., exchangeability permitting an internal permutation test) and the sample may come from the same task or other tasks, which can severely impact the test's power. I'm focusing on scenarios of calibration and statistical implications.\n\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1006,"totalTokenCount": 24346,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1006}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 23340,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "1P8Ca6WuO-PGxdIP2cO30Qk"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Statistical Scenarios**\n\nI'm now integrating insights from my investigation with literature on dependence testing in finite samples. Specifically, I'm focusing on the implications of identical marginals, with the minimum detectable gap in a scenario of $K$ independent Gaussian processes of length $n$ and $m$ reference steps. The minimax detectable difference on a scalar parameter scales as $\\sqrt{(Kn)^{-1}+m^{-1}}$, and for a Sobolev copula of regularity $s$, it becomes $((Kn)^{-1}+m^{-1})^{s/(2s+1)}$. I'm now also accounting for task shift bias $\\delta$, which creates an irreducible floor $\\Omega(\\delta)$. I'm further examining related works, including Sklar (1959), Neyman & Pearson (1933), Ingster (1993), and Vovk, Gammerman & Shafer (2005).\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1006,"totalTokenCount": 24697,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1006}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 23691,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "1P8Ca6WuO-PGxdIP2cO30Qk"}

